n generated by an LLM. Please report any inaccuracies to the developers. FLINT/Algorithms/Modular arithmetic\n\nAlgorithm\n\n# Multiplication by a fixed operand (Shoup)\n\nCost\n    One high product, two low products and one conditional subtraction per multiplication; one division per fixed operand.\n\nWhen the same a and n are used for many products ab \\bmod n, as in scaling a vector or applying a fixed twiddle factor in a number-theoretic transform, a quotient can be precomputed for a so that each product needs no division at all. The method was introduced in NTL by Shoup [Sho2021] and is analysed by Harvey [Har2014].\n\nThroughout, B = 2^{\\mathtt{FLINT\\_BITS}} and the modulus satisfies n < B/2. This restriction is what makes the final correction a single comparison.\n\n## Multiplication#\n\nThe precomputed data for 0 \\le a < n is \\hat a = \\lfloor aB/n \\rfloor, which fits in a word because a < n.   * `_nmod_vec_invert_shoup`, which builds the prefix products of Simultaneous inversion together with their precomputed quotients using Algorithm 2;\n  * scalar multiplication in `nmod_mat`, evaluation of polynomials at a point and at geometric progressions in `nmod_poly`, and modular multiplication by a fixed multiplier and Chinese remaindering in `mpn_extras`.\n\nOn SIMD hardware the same multiply-by-a-constant pattern is usually handled in floating point instead (Modular arithmetic in floating point).\n\n`ulong n_mulmod_shoup(ulong a, ulong b, ulong a_precomp, ulong n)`\n\n`void _nmod_vec_scalar_mul_nmod_shoup(nn_ptr res, nn_srcptr vec, slong len, ulong c, nmod_t mod)`\n\n`void _nmod_vec_invert_shoup(nn_ptr res, nn_srcptr vec, ulong len, nmod_t mod)`\n\n## References#\n\n  * [Sho2021] V. Shoup, \"Arithmetic software libraries\", chapter 9 of Computational Cryptography, Cambridge University Press (2021), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108854207.012\n  * [Har2014] D. Harvey, \"Faster arithmetic for number-theoretic transforms\", Journal of Symbolic Computation 60 (2